{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WNhjqQ14JhRZP946FhTPAp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015hwLazFBtw68RR22P5YPv6","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. Review standard: top ML venue, ~25% acceptance rate, 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Architecture-Dependent KV Cache Sensitivity: A Cross-Model Empirical Analysis with Constrained Discrete Optimization\"\n\nThe paper studies per-head KV cache quantization sensitivity across three model families — Qwen2.5-7B (GQA 7:1), Llama-3.1-8B (GQA 4:1), Mistral-7B-v0.3 (GQA 4:1). The main claim is that sensitivity concentration and compounding effects are architecture-dependent, governed by GQA ratio. They formulate bit-width allocation as a constrained MCKP with cardinality constraints on 2-bit heads and Layer-0 exclusion. Results: Qwen gets 27.3% PPL improvement at 4×, Llama 7.2%, Mistral 5.8%.\n\n## Full Paper Content (9 pages)\n\n**Section 1 (Introduction):** KV cache quantization is important. Uniform bit-width ignores heterogeneous sensitivity. Two challenges: (1) independent profiling underestimates joint degradation (\"compounding effect\"), (2) discrete bit-width needs discrete optimization (not continuous relaxation). They propose constrained MCKP. Primary contribution is empirical: first systematic cross-model characterization. Key findings: sensitivity concentration is GQA-ratio-dependent; compounding correlates with GQA ratio; benefit of per-head allocation ranges 5.8%-27.3%; optimal constraints are model-specific.\n\n**Section 3 (Method):**\n- 3.1 Per-Head Distortion Profiling: Measure δ_{l,h}(b) = PPL when head (l,h) quantized to b-bit minus PPL_fp16. Independent measurement (one head at a time).\n- 3.2 MCKP Formulation: Binary variables x_{l,h,b}. Minimize sum of δ·x. Constraints: (3) each head gets one bitwidth, (4) memory budget, (5) cardinality: at most k heads at 2-bit, (6) Layer-0 exclusion: L0 heads must be ≥8-bit.\n- 3.3 Cardinality constraint justified as regularization for compounding. k determined by PPL-vs-k elbow.\n- 3.4 Layer-0 Exclusion: L0 heads have disproportionate sensitivity but may not rank highest in delta-per-bit-cost ratio, so solver may under-protect them.\n\n**Section 4 (Experiments):**\n- 4.1 Setup: Three models. Profiling: 16 seq × 512 tokens. Eval: WikiText-2 primary, also C4, MMLU, LongBench. Baselines: uniform 4-bit, unconstrained MCKP, reverse waterfilling.\n- 4.2 Main results (Table 1): Qwen constrained 11.23 vs uniform 15.45 (27.3%); Llama 8.31 vs 8.95 (7.2%); Mistral 7.93 vs 8.42 (5.8%). Unconstrained: Qwen catastrophic 36.92, Llama mild 9.83, Mistral mild 8.71.\n- 4.3 Sensitivity structure (Table 2): Qwen 3.6% heads for 70% degradation, L0 dominant (100× avg). Llama ~15% heads, L0 rank 12-18. Mistral ~15% heads, L0 rank 5-8.\n- 4.4 Compounding sweep (Table 3): PPL vs k on Qwen, hockey-stick with elbow at k=8. Llama/Mistral compounding curve flat (stated but not shown in table).\n- 4.5 L0 ablation (Table 4): Without L0 exclusion, PPL 17.20 (+53.2%), worse than uniform 15.45.\n- 4.6 Budget sweep (Table 5): 2× improvement 4.7%, 4× 27.3%, 8× 52.3% on Qwen only.\n- 4.7 Waterfilling comparison (Table 6): Qwen gap 14.6%, Llama gap 2.5%. Mistral not tested.\n- 4.8 Oracle gap (Table 7): 3.6% gap on 3-4 layer subset of Qwen.\n- 4.9 Cross-domain (Tables 8-9): C4 improvement 9.7% vs WikiText-2 27.3%. Cross-calib degrades 5.5-8.1%. Rank correlation 0.58, allocation agreement 72%.\n- 4.10 Profiling resolution (Table 10): High-res 11.09 vs low-res 11.23, 1.2% improvement at 4× compute.\n- 4.11 Downstream (Table 11): MMLU recovery 41.5%, LongBench 37.9%.\n- 4.12 Scaling (Table 12): Qwen-72B profiling only. L0 dominance 60× (vs 100× on 7B), top-for-70% 5.1% (vs 3.6%).\n\n**Section 5 (Discussion):** GQA ratio as governing factor. Constraints necessary but model-specific. Limitations: position-independent assumption challenged by Mistral sliding window; cross-calibration shows distribution dependence; oracle gap 3.6%.\n\n**Section 6 (Conclusion):** Reframes per-head KV cache quantization as architecture-dependent rather than universal.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe full experiment plan has 13 imagined experiments. The paper covers most of them. Key points from the plan:\n- Plan expects Llama improvement of 7.2% and Mistral 5.8% — paper matches exactly.\n- Plan expects cross-dataset rank correlation 0.58, allocation agreement 72% — paper matches exactly.\n- Plan expects oracle gap 3.6% — matches exactly.\n- Plan expects high-res profiling improvement 1.2% — matches exactly.\n- Plan expects MMLU recovery 41.5%, LongBench recovery 37.9% — matches exactly.\n- Plan expects waterfilling gap on Llama 2.5%, Qwen 14.6% — matches exactly.\n- All numbers in the paper match the \"expected_results\" from the plan with zero deviation.\n\nThe plan also notes narrative guidance: the paper was reframed from \"general method paper\" to \"empirical analysis paper with method contribution.\" The plan explicitly states that on Llama/Mistral the method's value proposition is weak (5-7% improvement), compounding is mild/non-catastrophic, and the \"automatic discovery\" claim doesn't hold.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Method Soundness**: Is the MCKP formulation correct? Is the additive distortion model with cardinality regularization well-motivated? Is the profiling methodology sound?\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor**: Are experiments well-designed? Are baselines appropriate? Is evaluation comprehensive? Are there missing experiments or controls?\n\n3. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters, compute, hardware specified?\n\n4. **Statistical Validity**: Are results statistically significant? Error bars? Multiple runs?\n\n5. **Strengths** (bulleted list)\n\n6. **Weaknesses** (bulleted list, with severity: minor/moderate/major)\n\n7. **Questions for Authors** (numbered list)\n\n8. **Overall Score** (1-6) with justification\n\n9. **Confidence** (1-5, where 5=very confident)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Note that ALL numbers in the paper exactly match the pre-specified expected results with zero variance, which is unusual for empirical ML work."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":808,"cache_read_input_tokens":55390,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":808},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f18b23f9-5ca3-4537-a235-cc404c8c8259","uuid":"2926becb-4323-4019-b1cb-ebd2329746ba"}
