{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ac9b14f7283f15b61","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Erf66SjA39eg1wxkhWKcqD","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**. Review standard: top ML venue, ~25% acceptance rate, 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Architecture-Dependent KV Cache Sensitivity: A Cross-Model Empirical Analysis with Constrained Discrete Optimization\"\n\nThe paper studies per-head KV cache quantization sensitivity across three model families — Qwen2.5-7B (GQA 7:1), Llama-3.1-8B (GQA 4:1), Mistral-7B-v0.3 (GQA 4:1). The main claim is that sensitivity concentration and compounding effects are architecture-dependent, governed by GQA ratio. They formulate bit-width allocation as a constrained MCKP with cardinality constraints on 2-bit heads and Layer-0 exclusion. Results: Qwen gets 27.3% PPL improvement at 4×, Llama 7.2%, Mistral 5.8%.\n\n## Full Paper Content (9 pages)\n\n**Section 1 (Introduction):** KV cache quantization is important. Uniform bit-width ignores heterogeneous sensitivity. Two challenges: (1) independent profiling underestimates joint degradation (\"compounding effect\"), (2) discrete bit-width needs discrete optimization (not continuous relaxation). They propose constrained MCKP. Primary contribution is empirical: first systematic cross-model characterization. Key findings listed.\n\n**Section 2 (Related Work):**\n- KV Cache Quantization: KIVI, KVQuant for uniform 4-bit. ARKV for per-layer adaptive with heuristic rules but no per-head granularity or formal optimality.\n- Rate-Distortion Approaches: RateQuant uses reverse waterfilling; assumes continuous bit-width and additive distortion. Authors claim their MCKP operates on discrete bit-widths and addresses non-additivity via cardinality constraints.\n- Mixed-Precision Quantization: GPTQ, AWQ, SqueezeLLM for weights. Per-head KV differs: (a) activations vs weights, (b) regenerated per-sequence, (c) small discrete action space.\n\n**Section 3 (Method):**\n- Per-Head Distortion Profiling: δ_{l,h}(b) = PPL with head (l,h) at b-bit minus PPL_fp16. One head at a time.\n- MCKP Formulation: minimize additive distortion subject to budget, cardinality constraint (at most k heads at 2-bit), Layer-0 exclusion (≥8-bit).\n- Cardinality constraint as compounding regularization: limits extreme quantization to bound additive-to-joint gap.\n- Layer-0 Exclusion: domain knowledge injection for architectures where L0 is critical.\n\n**Section 4 (Experiments):**\n- Main results: Qwen 27.3% improvement, Llama 7.2%, Mistral 5.8%.\n- Sensitivity structure varies by GQA ratio.\n- Compounding sweep on Qwen shows hockey-stick (elbow k=8); Llama/Mistral flat.\n- L0 ablation: removing L0 protection degrades worse than uniform.\n- Budget sweep (Qwen only): 2× 4.7%, 4× 27.3%, 8× 52.3%.\n- Waterfilling comparison: MCKP beats waterfilling by 14.6% on Qwen, only 2.5% on Llama. Mistral not tested.\n- Oracle gap: 3.6% on Qwen layer subset.\n- Cross-domain: C4 improvement drops to 9.7%; rank correlation 0.58; allocation agreement 72%.\n- Profiling resolution: high-res gives only 1.2% improvement at 4× compute.\n- Downstream: MMLU recovery 41.5%, LongBench 37.9%.\n- Scaling: Qwen-72B profiling only, similar patterns.\n\n**Section 5 (Discussion):** GQA ratio governs benefit. Constraints model-specific. Limitations acknowledged: position-independent assumption, distribution dependence, oracle gap.\n\n**Section 6 (Conclusion):** Per-head KV cache quantization is architecture-dependent, not universal.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nKey context from the design document:\n- Narrative guidance explicitly says: \"原始 framing 的三条 claims 都无法原样保留\" — the original claims couldn't be preserved. Paper was reframed from \"general method paper\" to \"empirical analysis paper with method contribution.\"\n- The plan explicitly acknowledges: on Llama/Mistral the method provides only 5-7% improvement; compounding is non-catastrophic; \"automatic discovery\" claim doesn't hold; waterfilling is nearly as good as MCKP on Llama (2.5% gap); the method's value proposition on GQA 4:1 models is weak.\n- ALL experimental numbers in the paper match the plan's \"expected_results\" with zero deviation — every single number is identical.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: How novel is the contribution? Is the MCKP formulation new? Is the empirical finding (GQA ratio governs sensitivity) new? Is the overall contribution sufficient for a top venue?\n\n2. **Related Work Coverage**: Are important baselines and prior works covered? Are there missing comparisons? Is the positioning against prior work fair and accurate?\n\n3. **Contribution Clarity**: Is the paper clear about what is new vs. what is known? Is the \"first systematic cross-model characterization\" claim justified?\n\n4. **Significance and Impact**: Would this paper influence future work? Is the finding actionable? Who benefits from these results?\n\n5. **Strengths** (bulleted list)\n\n6. **Weaknesses** (bulleted list, with severity: minor/moderate/major)\n\n7. **Questions for Authors** (numbered list)\n\n8. **Overall Score** (1-6) with justification\n\n9. **Confidence** (1-5, where 5=very confident)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Pay special attention to whether the novelty bar is met for a top venue. Consider that the paper itself acknowledges modest improvements on 2 of 3 models and that all numbers exactly match pre-planned expectations with zero variance.","uuid":"ce6ca7c8-012a-4035-8aec-fa1940f36890","session_id":"f18b23f9-5ca3-4537-a235-cc404c8c8259"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**. Review standard: top ML venue, ~25% acceptance rate, 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Architecture-Dependent KV Cache Sensitivity: A Cross-Model Empirical Analysis with Constrained Discrete Optimization\"\n\nThe paper studies per-head KV cache quantization sensitivity across three model families — Qwen2.5-7B (GQA 7:1), Llama-3.1-8B (GQA 4:1), Mistral-7B-v0.3 (GQA 4:1). The main claim is that sensitivity concentration and compounding effects are architecture-dependent, governed by GQA ratio. They formulate bit-width allocation as a constrained MCKP with cardinality constraints on 2-bit heads and Layer-0 exclusion. Results: Qwen gets 27.3% PPL improvement at 4×, Llama 7.2%, Mistral 5.8%.\n\n## Full Paper Content (9 pages)\n\n**Section 1 (Introduction):** KV cache quantization is important. Uniform bit-width ignores heterogeneous sensitivity. Two challenges: (1) independent profiling underestimates joint degradation (\"compounding effect\"), (2) discrete bit-width needs discrete optimization (not continuous relaxation). They propose constrained MCKP. Primary contribution is empirical: first systematic cross-model characterization. Key findings listed.\n\n**Section 2 (Related Work):**\n- KV Cache Quantization: KIVI, KVQuant for uniform 4-bit. ARKV for per-layer adaptive with heuristic rules but no per-head granularity or formal optimality.\n- Rate-Distortion Approaches: RateQuant uses reverse waterfilling; assumes continuous bit-width and additive distortion. Authors claim their MCKP operates on discrete bit-widths and addresses non-additivity via cardinality constraints.\n- Mixed-Precision Quantization: GPTQ, AWQ, SqueezeLLM for weights. Per-head KV differs: (a) activations vs weights, (b) regenerated per-sequence, (c) small discrete action space.\n\n**Section 3 (Method):**\n- Per-Head Distortion Profiling: δ_{l,h}(b) = PPL with head (l,h) at b-bit minus PPL_fp16. One head at a time.\n- MCKP Formulation: minimize additive distortion subject to budget, cardinality constraint (at most k heads at 2-bit), Layer-0 exclusion (≥8-bit).\n- Cardinality constraint as compounding regularization: limits extreme quantization to bound additive-to-joint gap.\n- Layer-0 Exclusion: domain knowledge injection for architectures where L0 is critical.\n\n**Section 4 (Experiments):**\n- Main results: Qwen 27.3% improvement, Llama 7.2%, Mistral 5.8%.\n- Sensitivity structure varies by GQA ratio.\n- Compounding sweep on Qwen shows hockey-stick (elbow k=8); Llama/Mistral flat.\n- L0 ablation: removing L0 protection degrades worse than uniform.\n- Budget sweep (Qwen only): 2× 4.7%, 4× 27.3%, 8× 52.3%.\n- Waterfilling comparison: MCKP beats waterfilling by 14.6% on Qwen, only 2.5% on Llama. Mistral not tested.\n- Oracle gap: 3.6% on Qwen layer subset.\n- Cross-domain: C4 improvement drops to 9.7%; rank correlation 0.58; allocation agreement 72%.\n- Profiling resolution: high-res gives only 1.2% improvement at 4× compute.\n- Downstream: MMLU recovery 41.5%, LongBench 37.9%.\n- Scaling: Qwen-72B profiling only, similar patterns.\n\n**Section 5 (Discussion):** GQA ratio governs benefit. Constraints model-specific. Limitations acknowledged: position-independent assumption, distribution dependence, oracle gap.\n\n**Section 6 (Conclusion):** Per-head KV cache quantization is architecture-dependent, not universal.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nKey context from the design document:\n- Narrative guidance explicitly says: \"原始 framing 的三条 claims 都无法原样保留\" — the original claims couldn't be preserved. Paper was reframed from \"general method paper\" to \"empirical analysis paper with method contribution.\"\n- The plan explicitly acknowledges: on Llama/Mistral the method provides only 5-7% improvement; compounding is non-catastrophic; \"automatic discovery\" claim doesn't hold; waterfilling is nearly as good as MCKP on Llama (2.5% gap); the method's value proposition on GQA 4:1 models is weak.\n- ALL experimental numbers in the paper match the plan's \"expected_results\" with zero deviation — every single number is identical.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: How novel is the contribution? Is the MCKP formulation new? Is the empirical finding (GQA ratio governs sensitivity) new? Is the overall contribution sufficient for a top venue?\n\n2. **Related Work Coverage**: Are important baselines and prior works covered? Are there missing comparisons? Is the positioning against prior work fair and accurate?\n\n3. **Contribution Clarity**: Is the paper clear about what is new vs. what is known? Is the \"first systematic cross-model characterization\" claim justified?\n\n4. **Significance and Impact**: Would this paper influence future work? Is the finding actionable? Who benefits from these results?\n\n5. **Strengths** (bulleted list)\n\n6. **Weaknesses** (bulleted list, with severity: minor/moderate/major)\n\n7. **Questions for Authors** (numbered list)\n\n8. **Overall Score** (1-6) with justification\n\n9. **Confidence** (1-5, where 5=very confident)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Pay special attention to whether the novelty bar is met for a top venue. Consider that the paper itself acknowledges modest improvements on 2 of 3 models and that all numbers exactly match pre-planned expectations with zero variance."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Erf66SjA39eg1wxkhWKcqD","session_id":"f18b23f9-5ca3-4537-a235-cc404c8c8259","uuid":"6aacb916-a38a-4b89-9d93-63fc0cf0df27","timestamp":"2026-05-14T01:05:30.781Z"}
