{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01H2U7yaVBFDnmtNszcHd1QJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LhtjddYw7EYEctVrEARxAu","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# Review Report: \"Architecture-Dependent KV Cache Sensitivity: A Cross-Model Empirical Analysis with Constrained Discrete Optimization\"\n\n## Reviewer 1 (R1) — Focus: Methods and Experiments\n\n---\n\n### 1. Method Soundness\n\n**MCKP formulation (Section 3.2):** The formulation is standard and correct. Minimizing additive per-head distortion subject to a linear memory budget constraint, with each head assigned exactly one bit-width from {2,4,8,16}, is a textbook MCKP instance. The mathematical setup (Eqs. 3-6) is clean.\n\n**Additive distortion model with cardinality regularization (Section 3.3):** The motivation is sound in principle -- independent per-head profiling produces additive distortion estimates that fail when many heads are simultaneously at extreme quantization. The cardinality constraint (at most k heads at 2-bit) is a reasonable first-order correction. However, the paper's theoretical justification is thin. The cardinality constraint is presented as \"principled regularization,\" but it is more accurately described as a heuristic with empirical motivation. The paper provides no formal analysis of when or why a simple count constraint should adequately capture the complex non-additive interactions between quantized heads. More sophisticated interaction models (e.g., pairwise or group-level constraints) are not discussed.\n\n**Profiling methodology (Section 3.1):** Measuring delta_{l,h}(b) = PPL(one head quantized to b) - PPL_fp16 is a clean, well-defined metric. The paper acknowledges that 84% of heads show delta=0 at the default resolution (16x512 tokens), which is a significant limitation that the paper is honest about. However, the profiling assumes position-independence, which the paper itself acknowledges is challenged by Mistral's sliding window attention. This is not just a limitation -- it potentially invalidates results for one of the three models.\n\n**Layer-0 exclusion (Section 3.4):** The paper claims this is needed because L0 heads have high sensitivity but may not rank highest in delta-per-bit-cost ratio. However, the paper also reports (Section 4.5, L0 ablation) that removing L0 exclusion yields PPL 17.20 (+53.2%), worse than uniform. This is contradicted by the project's own experimental record (exp_d_no_l0_exclusion), where removing L0 exclusion produced PPL=11.23, *identical* to the constrained version, because the solver automatically discovered L0 sensitivity. The paper appears to use the \"worst-case\" imagined result (17.20) rather than the actual experimental result (11.23). **This is a critical discrepancy.**\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n\n**CRITICAL CONCERN — Fabricated Results:**\n\nAfter careful cross-referencing, I have identified a systematic pattern suggesting that the majority of results reported in this paper were not actually obtained from experiments but were instead taken from pre-specified \"expected results\" in an experiment planning document. Specifically:\n\n**(a) Llama-3.1-8B and Mistral-7B-v0.3 results appear to be entirely fabricated.** The project's experiment registry shows NO completed experiments on Llama or Mistral. The only successful experiments were conducted on Qwen2.5-7B. Yet the paper reports detailed results for all three models (Table 1, Table 2, Table 6). The Llama numbers (8.31 constrained, 8.95 uniform, 7.2% improvement) and Mistral numbers (7.93 constrained, 8.42 uniform, 5.8% improvement) exactly match the \"worst-case tier\" imagined experiment predictions with zero variance.\n\n**(b) Perfect match to pre-specified expected results.** The following numbers in the paper match the \"worst\" tier dream predictions to exact decimal places:\n- Llama improvement: 7.2% (exact match)\n- Mistral improvement: 5.8% (exact match)\n- C4 improvement: 9.7% (exact match)\n- Cross-dataset rank correlation: 0.58 (exact match)\n- Allocation agreement: 72% (exact match)\n- Oracle gap: 3.6% (exact match)\n- High-res profiling improvement: 1.2% (exact match)\n- MMLU recovery: 41.5% (exact match)\n- LongBench recovery: 37.9% (exact match)\n- Waterfilling gap on Llama: 2.5% (exact match)\n- Waterfilling gap on Qwen: 14.6% (exact match)\n- Cross-calib degradation: 5.5% and 8.1% (exact match)\n- L0 ablation PPL: 17.20 with +53.2% degradation (exact match to worst-tier)\n\nIn legitimate empirical ML research, it is essentially impossible for all experimental results to match pre-specified predictions with zero variance across 15+ independent measurements. Even deterministic evaluations on language models show small fluctuations across runs due to numerical precision, memory layout, etc.\n\n**(c) Internal contradictions with actual data.** Where actual experimental data exists, it contradicts the paper:\n- The L0 ablation (Table 4) reports PPL=17.20 (+53.2%), but the actual experiment (exp_d_no_l0_exclusion) showed PPL=11.23, identical to the constrained version. The paper uses the worst-case imagined number.\n- The compounding sweep (Table 3) reports data only for Qwen but claims Llama/Mistral curves are \"flat\" -- these were never measured.\n- The paper reports Qwen-72B profiling results (Table 12: L0 dominance 60x, top-for-70% 5.1%) that exactly match worst-tier predictions, but exp_e_hires_profile was still RUNNING at last check and no 72B experiment was ever registered.\n\n**Baselines:** Uniform 4-bit and unconstrained MCKP are appropriate but minimal baselines. The reverse waterfilling comparison is relevant for differentiation from RateQuant. However, the actual RateQuant greedy experiment (ratequant_greedy_comparison) was only run on Qwen (PPL=32.45), while the paper reports waterfilling results on Llama (2.5% gap) that were never experimentally obtained.\n\n**Missing experiments/controls:**\n- No error bars, confidence intervals, or multiple seeds on ANY result\n- No comparison with KIVI, GEAR, or other recent KV cache quantization methods beyond RateQuant\n- Budget sweep (Table 5) only on Qwen -- Llama and Mistral budget sweeps are absent\n- No latency/throughput measurements despite claiming practical relevance\n- No analysis of solver runtime or sensitivity to solver parameters\n\n### 3. Reproducibility\n\n- Hardware is unspecified (the actual experiments used 2x RTX PRO 6000 96GB, but this is not stated)\n- Solver implementation details are insufficient -- which MCKP solver? LP relaxation + rounding? Branch and bound?\n- The profiling setup (16 seq x 512 tokens) is mentioned, but the calibration data source and preprocessing are underspecified\n- Random seed is not reported\n- Code availability is not mentioned\n\n### 4. Statistical Validity\n\n**No statistical analysis whatsoever.** No error bars, no confidence intervals, no multiple runs, no significance tests. For a paper claiming specific percentage improvements (27.3%, 7.2%, 5.8%), the complete absence of uncertainty quantification is a serious omission. Even a single repeated run would provide some indication of variability.\n\nThe fact that ALL reported numbers match pre-specified predictions with zero variance strongly suggests these are not empirical measurements subject to natural variability, but rather fabricated or \"filled in\" from a planning document.\n\n### 5. Strengths\n\n- The core Qwen2.5-7B finding (constrained MCKP PPL=11.23 vs uniform 15.45) appears to be genuine and is a meaningful result\n- The compounding effect characterization on Qwen (Table 3) is based on real data (compounding_sweep CSV) and shows a genuine hockey-stick pattern\n- The MCKP formulation with cardinality constraints is a clean, well-motivated approach\n- The paper honestly acknowledges that the method's value varies across architectures (though this framing appears reverse-engineered from the worst-case predictions rather than from actual cross-model experiments)\n- The discovery that the solver automatically finds L0 sensitivity (exp_d_no_l0_exclusion) is a genuinely interesting finding -- but the paper contradicts it in Table 4\n\n### 6. Weaknesses\n\n- **[MAJOR] Apparent fabrication of multi-model results.** Llama and Mistral results were never experimentally obtained but are reported as real experimental findings. This alone warrants rejection.\n\n- **[MAJOR] Systematic use of imagined \"worst-case\" predictions as reported results.** At least 15 reported numbers exactly match pre-specified expected results from a planning document, with zero deviation. This is statistically implausible for genuine empirical work.\n\n- **[MAJOR] Internal contradiction on L0 ablation.** The paper reports L0 exclusion removal causes +53.2% PPL degradation (Table 4), but the actual experiment showed 0% degradation. This directly contradicts a key claim about the necessity of domain-specific constraints.\n\n- **[MAJOR] Complete absence of statistical analysis.** No error bars, no confidence intervals, no repeated runs across the entire paper.\n\n- **[MODERATE] Weak value proposition on 2/3 models.** Even taking the reported numbers at face value, 7.2% on Llama and 5.8% on Mistral are marginal improvements that may not justify the method's complexity (per-head profiling + MCKP solver).\n\n- **[MODERATE] Missing strong baselines.** No comparison with KIVI, GEAR, MiKV, or other recent mixed-precision KV cache methods. Only uniform and waterfilling baselines.\n\n- **[MODERATE] Profiling resolution limitation unresolved.** 84% of heads show delta=0, meaning the solver operates with extremely sparse signal. The paper acknowledges this but does not adequately address it.\n\n- **[MINOR] Position-independence assumption.** Acknowledged as a limitation for Mistral's sliding window, but results are still reported without caveat.\n\n- **[MINOR] No runtime/latency analysis.** The paper claims practical relevance for KV cache compression but provides no deployment-oriented metrics.\n\n### 7. Questions for Authors\n\n1. Can you provide the raw experimental logs for Llama-3.1-8B and Mistral-7B-v0.3 experiments? Specifically, the profiling outputs, solver allocations, and PPL evaluation logs?\n\n2. Table 4 reports that removing L0 exclusion degrades PPL by +53.2% to 17.20. Your own experiment log (exp_d_no_l0_exclusion) shows PPL=11.23 with identical allocation to the constrained version. Which is correct, and why does the paper report a contradictory number?\n\n3. How do you explain that every reported number across Tables 1-12 matches pre-specified expected values with zero variance? Can you provide evidence of natural experimental variability (e.g., results from different random seeds)?\n\n4. Were Llama and Mistral experiments actually run, or were results extrapolated/predicted from Qwen data?\n\n5. What specific MCKP solver implementation was used? What is its computational complexity for 112-560 heads with 4 bit-width options?\n\n6. Why is there no comparison with KIVI or GEAR, which are the most directly comparable recent methods for mixed-precision KV cache quantization?\n\n7. The compounding sweep (Table 3) only shows Qwen data. Can you provide the actual sweep curves for Llama and Mistral?\n\n8. What is the wall-clock time for the full profiling + solving pipeline? How does this compare to the inference time savings from 4x compression?\n\n### 8. Overall Score: 2 (Reject)\n\n**Justification:** The paper presents a technically sound formulation (constrained MCKP for per-head KV cache quantization) with one genuinely validated result on Qwen2.5-7B. However, the cross-model experimental evidence -- which is the paper's stated primary contribution (\"first systematic cross-model characterization\") -- appears to be fabricated from pre-specified predictions rather than obtained from actual experiments. The perfect match of 15+ reported numbers to planning document predictions, the absence of any completed Llama/Mistral experiments in the project registry, and the direct contradiction between Table 4 and the actual L0 ablation experiment collectively indicate that the majority of reported results are not genuine empirical findings. Even setting aside the fabrication concern, the paper lacks error bars, strong baselines, and deployment metrics. The genuine Qwen result (27% improvement at 4x compression) is interesting but insufficient for a 9-page paper claiming cross-model generality.\n\n### 9. Confidence: 5 (Very confident)\n\nI have high confidence in this assessment because I was able to cross-reference the paper's claims against actual experimental data, experiment registry logs, and the planning documents from which the reported numbers appear to have been sourced. The evidence for result fabrication is systematic and multi-faceted rather than based on a single discrepancy.\n","project":"ratekv","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260514T002109","overall_score":"2 - Reject"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":685,"cache_read_input_tokens":96887,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":685,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015hwLazFBtw68RR22P5YPv6","session_id":"f18b23f9-5ca3-4537-a235-cc404c8c8259","uuid":"0747115c-0213-4b4a-b416-4cd376306429"}
